\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Cell-incidence walks retain signs and transports}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Choose a fiber basis for the
\vocabk{invariant cochain pairing}{fdip-00SR}. Every power trace
#{\trace(\extD_U^\ell)} is a finite sum over closed walks
among the labeled cochain cells. Each step retains its exterior
incidence sign and its actual fiber map. In rank one, the
determinant permutation expansion of #{m\bbI+\extD_U}
contains cycle covers with short cycles and mass fixed points.}

\proof{
\p{For a cell label #{(x,I)} and #{i\notin I}, put
#{\epsilon(i,I)=(-1)^{|\{j\in I:j<i\}|}}.
The raising block contributing to the output label
#{(x,I\cup\{i\})} has two occurrences:
##{\begin{array}{c|c}
 \text{input label}&\text{fiber map}\\\hline
 (x+\varepsilon_i,I)&\epsilon(i,I)\rho(U_i(x))^{-1}\\
 (x,I)&-\epsilon(i,I)\bbI_F.
\end{array}}
The lowering occurrences are their adjoints, by
\ref{fdip-00SS}. Even when periodic endpoints coincide,
the two labeled occurrences remain separate summands.}

\p{Matrix multiplication selects a sequence of such
occurrences with matching cell labels. Its contribution is
the ordered product of the fiber maps, including all written
signs. A diagonal block closes the walk; its fiber trace then
contributes to the full trace. This is a finite block-matrix
expansion. The walks run among cells of adjacent cochain
degrees, so a bare physical plaquette product omits data.}

\p{For rank-one fibers, the determinant is the signed sum over
permutations of the cell labels. Decomposing each permutation
into cycles gives the cycle cover. Diagonal choices use the
mass, and two-cycles can use a raising occurrence and a
lowering occurrence. No restriction to long loops follows
from this determinant expansion.}
}
